<!-- ============ TESTING METHOD ============ -->
<h2>General testing method</h2>
<div class="disclaimer"><b>Before continuity testing:</b> isolate or de-energize the circuit first. Checking continuity on a live circuit gives false readings and can damage the meter.</div>
<div class="method">
<ul>
<li><b>Wire / strap / jumper:</b> should read near-zero Ω end to end. Anything above a few ohms means a loose crimp, corrosion, or a broken strand.</li>
<li><b>Resistor:</b> should read a stable, non-zero resistance across its two terminals. <b>0 Ω = shorted/burned</b>. <b>OL / infinite = open, likely burnt out</b>.</li>
<li><b>Relay contact:</b> near-zero Ω when the relay is in the state that should close that contact (picked for a front contact, relaxed for a back contact). Infinite when it should be closed means the contact isn't making up.</li>
<li><b>Relay coil:</b> should read a fixed, non-zero resistance matching the relay's rating. 0 Ω or OL both indicate a bad coil.</li>
<li><b>WAGO terminals:</b> probe the test port on top of the connector body, not the orange release lever. Backprobing at the adjoining screw terminal is a safer alternative.</li>
<li><b>Voltage vs continuity:</b> voltage checks are done live and tell you if a point is carrying power right now. Continuity checks are done dead and tell you if the conductor itself is physically intact.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<!-- ============ CONTACT TABLE ============ -->
<h2>Contact-by-contact continuity and voltage checks</h2>
<p class="lead">Work down this table in order. The first point that fails is where the fault lives.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Contact point</th><th>Continuity (power OFF)</th><th>Expect</th><th>Voltage (power ON)</th><th>Expect</th></tr>
<tr><td class="name">XPR contact<br>(term 21)</td><td>Lead A on XPR term 21, Lead B on the far end at 1-POR term 11. Disconnect one end first if possible.</td><td>Near-zero Ω when XPR relaxed. Open/OL when picked.</td><td>Red on XPR term 21, black on MN return.</td><td>Voltage present when relaxed; absent when picked.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">Terminal 11<br>(coil feed)</td><td>Lead A at term 11, Lead B back at XPR term 21.</td><td>Near-zero Ω.</td><td>Red at term 11, black on MN return.</td><td>Coil-rated DC voltage present if XPR closed &amp; wire good.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">1-POR coil</td><td>Across the coil's two terminals, isolated.</td><td>Stable non-zero Ω. 0 Ω = shorted. OL = open/burnt.</td><td>Red on coil+, black on coil-/MN.</td><td>Coil-rated voltage present; relay picked.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">2-POR coil</td><td>Across the coil's two terminals, isolated.</td><td>Stable non-zero Ω. 0 Ω = shorted. OL = open/burnt.</td><td>Red on coil+, black on coil-/MN.</td><td>Coil-rated voltage present; relay picked.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">1-POR contact<br>(term 5)</td><td>Across contact, relay held picked.</td><td>Near-zero Ω picked; open relaxed.</td><td>Red downstream side (toward 2-POR), black on MN.</td><td>Full upstream voltage passes when picked.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">2-POR contact<br>(term 5)</td><td>Across contact, relay held picked.</td><td>Near-zero Ω picked; open relaxed.</td><td>Red downstream side (toward POK), black on MN.</td><td>Full upstream voltage passes when picked.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">POK wire →<br>WAGO 20+</td><td>Lead A at 2-POR term 5 output, Lead B at WAGO 20+ test port.</td><td>Near-zero Ω.</td><td>Red at WAGO 20+ test port, black on MN.</td><td>Present if all above check out. If suspect, disconnect lamp and re-check.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">PO lamp</td><td>Across lamp leads, disconnected.</td><td>Measurable resistance or diode reading; OL = burnt.</td><td>N/A — covered by WAGO 20+ check above.</td><td>N/A</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ============ RESISTOR TABLE ============ -->
<h2>Lamp resistor block &amp; straps</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Element</th><th>Terminals</th><th>Check</th><th>Expect</th></tr>
<tr><td class="name">RG3T resistor</td><td>Top: RG3T POK-R/POK-L<br>Bottom: RG3B/1-POR 8</td><td>Resistance top-to-bottom</td><td>Stable non-zero Ω</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">RG6T/B resistor</td><td>Top: RG6B XN/LD45T<br>Bottom: RG6T POK-L XN/POK-R XN</td><td>Resistance top-to-bottom</td><td>Stable non-zero Ω</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">RF6T/B resistor</td><td>Top: RF6B XN/LD45T<br>Bottom: RF6T XN POK</td><td>Resistance top-to-bottom</td><td>Stable non-zero Ω</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">RF3T resistor</td><td>Top: RF3T POK-S<br>Bottom: RF3B/2-POR 3</td><td>Resistance top-to-bottom</td><td>Stable non-zero Ω</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">Strap 1</td><td>RG6T POK-L XN ↔ POK-R XN</td><td>Continuity across shared leads</td><td>Near-zero Ω</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name">Strap 2</td><td>RG6B XN/LD45T ↔ RF6B XN/LD45T</td><td>Continuity across the jumper</td><td>Near-zero Ω</td></tr>
<tr class="cut"><td class="name">Strap 3 <span class="badge">should be open</span></td><td>RF6B XN/LD45T ↔ RF6T XN POK</td><td>Continuity across the jumper</td><td>Should read OPEN — documented as open. Continuous = unexpected, flag it.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ============ SPECIAL NOTES ============ -->
<h2>Special notes</h2>
<h3>WAGO 20+ appears to have voltage from the lamp side</h3>
<p class="lead" style="text-align:left;max-width:720px;">A lamp cannot generate power and feed it backward into a dead circuit — it's a passive load. An apparent voltage reading here while upstream is confirmed dead is most often a meter ghost/phantom reading from induced voltage on a de-energized conductor bundled near live wires. Use a low-impedance (LoZ) meter setting if available. To confirm: disconnect the lamp from WAGO 20+ and re-check. Reading disappears → it was a ghost, real fault is upstream. Reading remains → the point does have real power and the fault is at the lamp itself.</p>
<h3>Two wires of opposite polarity on the same terminal</h3>
<p class="lead" style="text-align:left;max-width:720px;">Not normal practice — don't assume it's correct. Trace both wires back to their actual source before concluding it's a fault: the terminal may be a common tie point for same-polarity wires, or one wire may be a pass-through strap to another terminal rather than truly terminating there.</p>

</body>
</html>
